---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const post = {
 title: 'ANSI 107 Class 2 vs Class 3: A Planning Guide for Las Vegas Crews',
 description:
 "An educational comparison of Class 2, Class 3, and Class E hi-vis for Las Vegas crews. Employers must select apparel from the hazard assessment and current requirements.",
 category: 'Compliance',
 readTime: '9 min read',
 date: 'Apr 28, 2026',
 isoDate: '2026-04-28',
 image: '/images/generated/workwear-flatlay.jpg',
 imageAlt: 'Representative high-visibility apparel shown for employer planning',
 slug: 'ansi-107-class-2-vs-class-3-hi-vis-las-vegas',
}

const schema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@graph': [
 {
 '@type': 'Article',
 headline: post.title,
 description: post.description,
 image: `https://bighornthreads.com${post.image}`,
 datePublished: post.isoDate,
 dateModified: '2026-08-21',
 author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/about/' },
 publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/images/logos/bighorn-logo-color.png' } },
 mainEntityOfPage: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 },
 {
 '@type': 'FAQPage',
 mainEntity: [
 { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is the difference between ANSI 107 Class 2 and Class 3?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Class 3 has greater visibility requirements than Class 2. The employer selects the class from the hazard assessment, work-zone rules, and current ANSI/ISEA 107 guidance. Speed or shift alone should not be used as a universal selection rule.' } },
 { '@type': 'Question', name: 'When is Class E used?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Class E applies to supplemental high-visibility garments such as pants. The employer must confirm whether a Class E item and upper-body garment form an acceptable ensemble for the work.' } },
 { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can ANSI 107 hi-vis be embroidered or screen-printed?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Possibly. The employer and garment manufacturer must approve the proposed method and placement against the current product documentation before decoration.' } },
 ],
 },
 ],
}
---

<BaseLayout title={post.title} description={post.description} canonical={`https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`} ogImage={post.image} type="article" publishedDate={post.isoDate}>
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

 <article class="bg-concrete">
 <header class="border-b border-hairline bg-paper">
 <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pt-20 pb-12 sm:px-6 sm:pt-24 lg:px-8">
 <nav class="mb-6 font-mono text-xs text-ash" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
 <a href="/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Home</a><span class="px-2">/</span><a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Blog</a>
 </nav>
 <span class="mb-3 inline-block text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">{post.category}</span>
 <h1 class="mb-6 font-heading text-3xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">{post.title}</h1>
 <p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-ash">
 An educational overview of the classes and questions to take to your safety lead. The employer's hazard assessment and current project requirements control selection.
 </p>
 <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-t border-hairline pt-6 font-mono text-sm text-ash">
 <span class="text-gold-600">Bighorn Threads Team</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span>{post.readTime}</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><time datetime={post.isoDate}>{post.date}</time>
 </div>
 </div>
 </header>

 <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pt-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
 <img src={post.image} alt={post.imageAlt} width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" class="w-full border border-hairline object-cover" />
 </div>

 <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 ANSI/ISEA 107 defines performance classes for high-visibility safety apparel. Project rules may reference the standard. The employer's hazard assessment and current requirements control the selection.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Bighorn Threads coordinates hi-vis apparel programs for road contractors, paving crews, traffic control, utility workers, and signal techs. Your employer selects the required class and garment. Bighorn then coordinates approved decoration through a production partner. See our <a href="/custom-safety-vests-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline hover:text-gold-700">custom safety vests Las Vegas</a> page for product options.
 </p>

 <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Class 2: A Mid-Level Visibility Class</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Class 2 garments provide more visible material than Class 1 garments. Product designs include vests, shirts, and polos. Confirm the exact garment's current label and manufacturer documentation.
 </p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Class 2 may apply to some work around managed traffic, but the employer's hazard assessment controls the choice. Product options include the ML Kishigo 9100 series mesh-back tee, the 1191/1193 Premium Black Series polo, and the Radians ST21 mesh tee. Compare current manufacturer specifications before ordering.
 </p>

 <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Class 3: Greater Worker Visibility</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Class 3 has greater visibility requirements than Class 2. Garment designs provide visibility through the worker's range of motion. The employer selects Class 3 when the hazard assessment and applicable work-zone requirements call for it.
 </p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Class 3 may apply to roadway, utility, emergency-response, or low-visibility work. Contract and traffic-control requirements can also affect selection. Compare the current labels for products such as ML Kishigo shirts or jackets before ordering.
 </p>

 <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Class E: Hi-Vis Pants</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Class E is a supplemental classification used for garments such as pants. Some Class E and upper-body garment combinations may form a higher-class ensemble. Verify the exact combination against current labels and employer requirements before use.
 </p>

 <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Decoration Review for Hi-Vis Apparel</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Decoration can affect the visible background and retroreflective areas needed for a garment's class. Do not assume a placement is acceptable. Use the current manufacturer instructions and obtain employer approval for the exact method and placement.
 </p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Bighorn coordinates only employer-approved products and decoration requests through production partners. Before production, the employer and garment manufacturer must approve the proposed method and placement. Documentation availability depends on the manufacturer and partner and is confirmed in writing.
 </p>

 <h2 class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Questions for Your Safety Lead</h2>
 <ul class="mb-8 ml-6 list-disc space-y-2 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li>Which performance class does the hazard assessment require?</li>
 <li>Which contract, traffic-control, or site rules apply?</li>
 <li>Does the selected SKU carry the required current label?</li>
 <li>Will a proposed garment combination form the required ensemble?</li>
 <li>How will hot-weather comfort be addressed without changing the approved product?</li>
 <li>Does another hazard require a separately rated garment or protective system?</li>
 </ul>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 For deeper coverage on hi-vis selection and Nevada-specific compliance, see our <a href="/safety-compliance/ansi-107-hi-vis-guide/" class="text-gold-600 underline hover:text-gold-700">ANSI 107 hi-vis selection guide</a> and the <a href="/blog/nevada-osha-heat-rules-2026-workwear/" class="text-gold-600 underline hover:text-gold-700">Nevada OSHA heat rules 2026</a> post for how the new heat standard affects hi-vis fabric selection in Vegas summer.
 </p>

 <div class="my-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-8">
 <h3 class="mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Need a quote for employer-selected hi-vis?</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg text-ash">
 Send the approved product specification and decoration request. Documentation availability and production terms are confirmed in writing.
 </p>
 <a href="/get-a-quote/" class="inline-flex min-h-[48px] items-center justify-center bg-gold-500 px-7 py-4 text-base font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-charcoal transition-all hover:bg-gold-600">Request a Hi-Vis Quote</a>
 </div>
 </div>
 </article>
</BaseLayout>
